  <h2 id="why-curate">Why a curated ready-stock collection works for buyers</h2>
  <p>
    Ready stock can reduce the development work required to start or refresh an activewear range because the base silhouettes already exist. The value is not simply speed. A curated selection helps a buyer test a connected product story: a bra can pair with leggings, a jacket can layer over a set, and a color family can give the store a more intentional presentation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The selection should be designed around buyer decisions, not just garment categories. A core legging answers the repeat-purchase need. A matching set improves outfit value. A sports bra or top gives customers a choice of coverage. A light jacket, tennis skirt or capri style can provide a seasonal or trend-led reason to browse again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="performance">Select performance by product use, not by marketing language</h2>
  <p>
    “High performance” should mean that the garment has a clear job for the wearer. For a yoga legging, that may be comfortable movement, stable recovery and coverage appropriate to the selected color and fit. For a sports bra, support comes from more than fabric: the underband, strap arrangement, lining and pattern matter. For a light workout jacket, mobility, layer weight and pocket placement may matter most.
  </p>
  <div class="catalog-table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Performance questions to ask</th><th>Practical buyer check</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Yoga leggings</strong></td><td>How does the fabric stretch and recover? How does the waistband feel in movement?</td><td>Review the actual color and fit through bend, squat and lunge movements.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sports bras</strong></td><td>What activity and support level is the silhouette designed for?</td><td>Check underband stability, strap comfort, coverage and the target size range.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Matching sets</strong></td><td>Do the top and bottom create a clear outfit without limiting movement?</td><td>Try the complete set and confirm that the colors, fabric and proportions work together.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Light layers</strong></td><td>Will the jacket or top layer easily over the core outfit?</td><td>Check sleeve movement, zipper or pocket details and the product’s intended climate.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Courtwear and skorts</strong></td><td>Does the design meet the intended activity and coverage expectation?</td><td>Check inner-short construction, movement and how the set pairs with bras or tops.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="comfort">Comfort starts with the finished garment, not one fabric label</h2>
  <p>
    Buyers often ask for a “soft, premium” activewear fabric. That direction is useful, but it should become a concrete sample review. Fabric composition, GSM, knit construction, stretch, recovery and finishing all influence hand feel. Seams, grading and the garment pattern determine whether that fabric stays comfortable after it is worn.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Feel the fabric in the intended product:</strong> a fabric card is a starting point, but a finished legging, bra or jacket gives a more reliable comfort impression.</li>
    <li><strong>Review color and coverage together:</strong> light colors, high-stretch zones and different size grades should be considered in the final garment.</li>
    <li><strong>Check recovery:</strong> the product should return to its intended shape after movement and a practical wash review.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the capsule wearable:</strong> customers often return for pieces that layer, match and work beyond a single workout context.</li>
  </ul>

  <figure class="catalog-detail-image">
    <img src="/images/blogs/curated-ready-stock-activewear-collection/custom-activewear-solutions-catalog-showroom.webp" alt="Activewear catalog pages in a Fitclo showroom showing matching sports bra and biker short color options" width="960" height="1280" loading="lazy" />
